  {
    "title": "Plato's Allegory of the Cave",
    "category": "philosophy-psychology",
    "subject": "Philosophy",
    "topic": "Plato's Republic",
    "passage": [
      "In Book VII of the Republic, Plato has Socrates describe prisoners chained since childhood in a cave, facing a wall. Behind them a fire burns, and between the fire and the prisoners people carry objects whose shadows fall on the wall. The prisoners take the shadows for the whole of reality and compete to predict which shadow will come next.",
      "One prisoner is freed and turned toward the fire. The light hurts his eyes, and he at first believes the shadows are more real than the objects that cast them. Dragged up out of the cave, he is dazzled by the sun, and only slowly learns to see reflections, then things themselves, and finally the sun, which he understands as the cause of the seasons and of everything visible.",
      "If he returns to the cave, his eyes, used to the light, see poorly in the dark. The prisoners laugh at him, conclude that the journey upward ruined his sight, and, Socrates suggests, might kill anyone who tried to free them. Readers often hear an echo of Socrates' own trial and death.",
      "Plato connects the image to the analogies of the sun and the divided line just before it. The cave stands for the world of sense experience and opinion; the world outside stands for the intelligible realm of the Forms, the unchanging realities that ordinary things only imitate; and the sun stands for the Form of the Good, which makes the Forms knowable. Education, Socrates says, is not putting sight into blind eyes but turning the whole soul toward the light.",
      "The allegory also has a political point. The philosophers who have seen the Good must be compelled to go back down and rule, because they alone know what is truly good for the city, and because those who do not want to rule are the least likely to rule for their own gain.",
      "Interpreters disagree about how far to take it. Some read it as a strict theory of knowledge that ranks the senses below pure reason. Others read it mainly as a picture of education as painful conversion. Critics, including Karl Popper, see in the ruling philosopher a warrant for paternalism or authoritarian rule by those who claim superior insight. Modern readers often apply the cave to propaganda, advertising or screens, and argue over whether that stretches Plato's meaning or captures it."
    ],
    "objectives": [
