        "facts": ["The shadows and the cave stand for the world of sense and opinion.", "The world outside stands for the intelligible realm of the Forms, and the sun for the Form of the Good.", "The image follows the sun and divided line analogies in the Republic.", "A strong answer weighs whether experience is merely misleading or a necessary first stage of learning."],
        "misconceptions": ["The allegory says everything we see is a hallucination.", "Plato thought the senses tell us nothing at all."] },
      { "key": "education-as-turning", "title": "Judge Plato's picture of education", "description": "Argues whether education is a painful turning of the soul or the transfer of information, and what that implies for teaching.",
        "concept": "education-as-conversion", "definition": "Socrates' claim that education turns the soul toward what is real rather than putting knowledge into it.",
        "facts": ["The freed prisoner is first pained and confused and prefers the shadows.", "His sight adjusts gradually, from reflections to things to the sun.", "Socrates denies that education puts sight into blind eyes.", "A defensible view addresses whether learners must be compelled, as the prisoner is dragged upward."],
