    "title": "Aristotle's Virtue Ethics",
    "category": "philosophy-psychology",
    "subject": "Philosophy",
    "topic": "Virtue ethics",
    "passage": [
      "In the Nicomachean Ethics, Aristotle asks what the highest human good is. Everyone agrees it is eudaimonia, usually translated happiness or flourishing, but people disagree about what it consists in: pleasure, honour or wealth. Aristotle argues that the good for a thing depends on its function, and that the distinctive human function is activity of the soul in accordance with reason. Eudaimonia is therefore rational activity in accordance with virtue, over a complete life.",
      "Virtue (arete, excellence) is a stable state of character. Aristotle distinguishes virtues of thought, such as practical wisdom (phronesis), from virtues of character, such as courage, temperance, generosity and justice. Virtues of character are not inborn; we acquire them by habituation, becoming just by doing just acts and brave by doing brave acts, much as we become builders by building.",
      "Each virtue of character is a mean between two vices, one of excess and one of deficiency. Courage lies between rashness and cowardice; generosity between wastefulness and stinginess. The mean is relative to us and to the situation, not an arithmetic midpoint, and it is found by the person of practical wisdom. Some actions and feelings, such as murder or spite, have no mean at all.",
      "Aristotle also holds that flourishing needs some external goods: friends, health, a measure of wealth, and good fortune. A person who suffers terrible misfortunes, like Priam at the fall of Troy, cannot be called happy, though a virtuous person will bear misfortune nobly.",
      "Modern virtue ethics, revived by philosophers such as Elizabeth Anscombe, Philippa Foot and Alasdair MacIntyre, presents itself as an alternative to rule-based theories. Instead of asking which rule an act obeys or what outcome it produces, it asks what a virtuous person would do and what kind of person one is becoming.",
      "Critics reply that \"do what the virtuous person would do\" gives little guidance in hard cases, that the list of virtues reflects one culture (Aristotle's ideal included pride and excluded humility, and he defended slavery and denied women full rationality), and that tying happiness to luck seems unfair. Defenders answer that practical wisdom is exactly the skill of handling cases no rule anticipates, and that the core idea can be separated from Aristotle's prejudices."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "eudaimonia", "title": "Is happiness the same as flourishing?", "description": "Defends or challenges Aristotle's function argument and his claim that the good life is virtuous rational activity.",
        "concept": "eudaimonia", "definition": "Flourishing or living well: for Aristotle, rational activity in accordance with virtue over a complete life.",
        "facts": ["Aristotle rejects pleasure, honour and wealth as the highest good.", "The function argument says the human good is activity in accordance with reason.", "Eudaimonia is an activity over a whole life, not a passing feeling.", "A strong answer asks whether humans really have a single function."],
        "misconceptions": ["Eudaimonia just means feeling happy at the moment.", "Aristotle said the good life is whatever you enjoy most."] },
      { "key": "doctrine-of-mean", "title": "Is virtue really a mean?", "description": "Tests the doctrine of the mean on examples and judges whether it explains virtue or only describes it.",
        "concept": "doctrine-of-the-mean", "definition": "Each virtue of character lies between a vice of excess and a vice of deficiency, relative to us and the situation.",
        "facts": ["Courage lies between rashness and cowardice.", "The mean is relative to us, not an arithmetic midpoint.", "Some actions, such as murder, have no mean.", "A strong answer considers virtues like honesty or justice that seem hard to place on a scale."],
        "misconceptions": ["The mean means always being moderate and never feeling strongly.", "Aristotle meant the exact halfway point between two extremes."] },
      { "key": "habituation", "title": "Can character be trained?", "description": "Judges the claim that we become virtuous by practice, and what follows for moral education and responsibility.",
        "concept": "habituation", "definition": "Aristotle's view that virtues of character are acquired by repeatedly doing virtuous acts.",
        "facts": ["Virtues of character are not inborn but formed by habit.", "We become just by doing just acts, as builders become builders by building.", "Practical wisdom is needed to know which act is right in a situation.", "A strong answer distinguishes acting from habit from acting merely out of routine."],
        "misconceptions": ["Doing one good deed makes a person virtuous.", "If character comes from upbringing, no one is responsible for it."] },
      { "key": "guidance-objection", "title": "Does virtue ethics tell us what to do?", "description": "Weighs the objection that virtue ethics gives no guidance in dilemmas against the defence from practical wisdom.",
        "concept": "action-guidance", "definition": "The objection that 'act as the virtuous person would' cannot decide hard cases.",
        "facts": ["Virtue ethics asks what kind of person to be, not only which rule to follow.", "Critics say it gives little guidance when virtues conflict.", "Defenders reply that practical wisdom handles cases rules cannot anticipate.", "Aristotle's own list of virtues reflects his culture, including his defence of slavery."],
        "misconceptions": ["Virtue ethics has no view about right action at all.", "Because Aristotle held prejudiced views, his whole theory is worthless."] }
    ],
    "translations": {
