    "title": "Stoicism and What We Control",
    "category": "philosophy-psychology",
    "subject": "Philosophy",
    "topic": "Hellenistic ethics",
    "passage": [
      "Stoicism was founded in Athens around 300 BCE by Zeno of Citium and taught in the Painted Porch (stoa) from which it takes its name. Its best-known later voices are Roman: the statesman Seneca, the former slave Epictetus, and the emperor Marcus Aurelius, whose private notebook we call the Meditations.",
      "Epictetus' Handbook opens with a distinction: some things are up to us and some are not. Up to us are our judgments, intentions, desires and aversions; not up to us are our body, property, reputation and office. Suffering comes from treating what is not up to us as if it were. The Stoic aims to want only what is in their power.",
      "The Stoics held that virtue, which is wisdom expressed as courage, justice and moderation, is the only true good, and vice the only true evil. Health, wealth and reputation are \"indifferents\": some are preferred and reasonably pursued, but none can make a life good or bad. A good person can therefore flourish even in poverty, exile or prison.",
      "Emotions such as fear, grief and anger were, for the Stoics, mistaken judgments: believing that something not up to us is truly good or bad. The goal was apatheia, freedom from these passions, not numbness. The wise person still has \"good feelings\" such as joy and caution, and the Stoics placed great weight on duty to family, city and all humanity, whom they saw as fellow citizens of one cosmos.",
      "Stoic practices included imagining losses in advance, reviewing one's day, and reminding oneself that everything passes. Modern cognitive behavioural therapy credits Epictetus' remark that people are disturbed not by things but by their judgments about things.",
      "Critics have long objected. Some say Stoicism counsels passivity in the face of injustice: if a tyrant or an unjust system is not up to me, should I simply accept it? Others say it underrates grief and love, since mourning a child seems fitting rather than mistaken. Defenders reply that the Stoics were active in public life and that the point is to act fully while accepting the outcome. A further debate concerns where to draw the line, since much of what affects us, from health to social conditions, is partly up to us."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "dichotomy-of-control", "title": "Where is the line of control?", "description": "Interprets Epictetus' distinction and tests whether things like health, success or relationships fall clearly on one side.",
        "concept": "dichotomy-of-control", "definition": "Epictetus' division between what is up to us (judgments, intentions, desires) and what is not (body, property, reputation).",
        "facts": ["Epictetus places judgments, desires and aversions within our control.", "Body, property, reputation and office are not up to us.", "Suffering comes from treating what is not up to us as ours.", "A strong answer deals with things that are partly in our control."],
        "misconceptions": ["Stoicism means you control nothing, so nothing matters.", "Anything you can influence at all is fully in your control."] },
      { "key": "virtue-only-good", "title": "Is virtue the only true good?", "description": "Defends or challenges the Stoic claim that health, wealth and reputation are indifferent to living well.",
        "concept": "preferred-indifferents", "definition": "Things like health and wealth that are reasonably preferred but cannot make a life good or bad.",
        "facts": ["The Stoics held virtue is the only good and vice the only evil.", "Health and wealth are preferred indifferents, not worthless.", "On this view a good person can flourish in poverty or prison.", "A strong answer weighs this against Aristotle's view that flourishing needs external goods."],
        "misconceptions": ["Stoics thought money and health should be rejected.", "Calling something indifferent means it should be ignored completely."] },
      { "key": "emotions-as-judgments", "title": "Are emotions mistaken judgments?", "description": "Judges the Stoic theory of the passions, including whether grief or anger can be fitting.",
        "concept": "apatheia", "definition": "Freedom from passions, which the Stoics saw as false judgments that something outside our control is good or bad.",
        "facts": ["The Stoics treated fear, grief and anger as false judgments of value.", "Apatheia is freedom from passions, not the absence of all feeling.", "The wise person still has good feelings such as joy.", "Cognitive behavioural therapy draws on the idea that judgments shape distress."],
        "misconceptions": ["Stoics wanted people to feel nothing at all.", "Being stoic just means hiding your emotions."] },
      { "key": "passivity-objection", "title": "Does Stoicism counsel passivity?", "description": "Weighs the objection that Stoicism accepts injustice against the Stoic emphasis on duty and action.",
        "concept": "acceptance-and-action", "definition": "The question of whether accepting outcomes outside our control undermines the will to change the world.",
        "facts": ["Seneca and Marcus Aurelius held high public office.", "The Stoics saw all humans as citizens of one cosmos with duties to each other.", "Critics say accepting what is not up to us can excuse injustice.", "A strong answer separates accepting an outcome from not trying to change it."],
        "misconceptions": ["Stoicism tells you never to try to change anything.", "Acceptance is the same as approval."] }
    ],
    "translations": {
      "ko": { "title": "스토아주의와 우리가 통제할 수 있는 것",
        "objectives": { "dichotomy-of-control": "통제의 경계는 어디인가?", "virtue-only-good": "덕만이 참된 선인가?", "emotions-as-judgments": "감정은 잘못된 판단인가?", "passivity-objection": "스토아주의는 수동성을 권하는가?" } },
      "es": { "title": "El estoicismo y lo que depende de nosotros",
        "objectives": { "dichotomy-of-control": "¿Dónde está el límite de lo que controlamos?", "virtue-only-good": "¿Es la virtud el único bien verdadero?", "emotions-as-judgments": "¿Son las emociones juicios equivocados?", "passivity-objection": "¿Aconseja el estoicismo la pasividad?" } }
