    "title": "Existentialism: Sartre and Camus",
    "category": "philosophy-psychology",
    "subject": "Philosophy",
    "topic": "Existentialism",
    "passage": [
      "Existentialism is a broad movement, rooted in Kierkegaard and Nietzsche and flourishing in France after the Second World War, that starts from the situation of the individual who must choose how to live without a guaranteed meaning.",
      "In his 1945 lecture Existentialism Is a Humanism, Jean-Paul Sartre summed up his view as \"existence precedes essence\". A paper knife is made to a plan, so its essence comes first. If there is no God who designed human nature, then human beings first exist and only afterwards define themselves by what they do. We are, Sartre said, \"condemned to be free\": not choosing is itself a choice.",
      "With freedom comes responsibility, and with it anguish. Sartre argued that in choosing for myself I also affirm an image of what a human being should be. Bad faith (mauvaise foi) is the evasion of this freedom: pretending that our role, our past or our nature forces us to act. His example is a café waiter who plays at being a waiter as if that were all he could be.",
      "Albert Camus, though often grouped with Sartre, rejected the label. In The Myth of Sisyphus (1942) he described the absurd: the clash between our demand for meaning and a universe that answers with silence. He rejected physical suicide and also \"philosophical suicide\", the leap into a faith or system that dissolves the tension. Instead he urged revolt: living with full awareness of the absurd. Sisyphus, condemned to roll his rock up a hill forever, becomes his hero: \"One must imagine Sisyphus happy.\"",
      "The two disagreed politically. After Camus' The Rebel (1951) criticized revolutionary violence and the Soviet camps, a hostile review in Sartre's journal Les Temps modernes led to a public break in 1952. Sartre held that one must commit to history and its struggles; Camus insisted on limits that no cause may cross.",
      "Critics ask whether radical freedom is believable given the weight of upbringing, class, body and circumstance; Simone de Beauvoir and the later Sartre gave more weight to such situations. Others ask whether, without a given human nature, existentialism can say that any choice is better than another, or whether Camus' cheerful Sisyphus is a real answer or a rhetorical flourish."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "existence-precedes-essence", "title": "Do we define ourselves?", "description": "Interprets 'existence precedes essence' and judges whether there is a fixed human nature.",
        "concept": "existence-precedes-essence", "definition": "Sartre's claim that humans exist first and create their essence through their choices.",
        "facts": ["Sartre contrasts a designed paper knife with human beings.", "He ties the claim to the absence of a designing God.", "We are 'condemned to be free'; refusing to choose is a choice.", "A strong answer weighs biology, upbringing and circumstance against radical freedom."],
        "misconceptions": ["Existentialism says you can become literally anything you want.", "Existence precedes essence means nothing about you is real."] },
      { "key": "bad-faith", "title": "Is bad faith avoidable?", "description": "Explains bad faith with an example and judges whether anyone can live fully without it.",
        "concept": "bad-faith", "definition": "Self-deception by which we deny our freedom, pretending our role or nature forces our choices.",
        "facts": ["Sartre's café waiter plays at being a waiter.", "Bad faith blames roles, the past or nature for choices.", "Freedom brings responsibility and anguish.", "A strong answer distinguishes real constraint from excuses."],
        "misconceptions": ["Bad faith just means lying to other people.", "Having a job or a social role is itself bad faith."] },
      { "key": "the-absurd", "title": "How should we respond to the absurd?", "description": "Interprets Camus' absurd and judges his choice of revolt over suicide and over a leap of faith.",
        "concept": "the-absurd", "definition": "Camus' name for the clash between the human demand for meaning and the silence of the universe.",
        "facts": ["The absurd lies in the relation between us and the world, not in the world alone.", "Camus rejects physical suicide and 'philosophical suicide'.", "He urges revolt: living in full awareness of the absurd.", "A strong answer says whether 'imagine Sisyphus happy' is convincing."],
        "misconceptions": ["Camus said life is meaningless, so nothing matters.", "The absurd means the world itself is irrational."] },
      { "key": "sartre-camus-split", "title": "Who was right in the Sartre-Camus split?", "description": "Weighs Sartre's commitment to history against Camus' insistence on moral limits to revolt.",
        "concept": "commitment-and-limits", "definition": "The 1952 quarrel over whether political commitment can justify revolutionary violence.",
        "facts": ["Camus' The Rebel criticized revolutionary violence and the Soviet camps.", "A hostile review in Les Temps modernes led to their public break in 1952.", "Sartre stressed engagement with historical struggle; Camus stressed limits no cause may cross.", "A strong answer connects the quarrel to each thinker's view of freedom and meaning."],
        "misconceptions": ["Sartre and Camus held the same philosophy.", "Camus was simply apolitical."] }
    ],
    "translations": {
      "ko": { "title": "실존주의: 사르트르와 카뮈",
        "objectives": { "existence-precedes-essence": "우리는 스스로를 규정하는가?", "bad-faith": "자기기만은 피할 수 있는가?", "the-absurd": "부조리에 어떻게 대응해야 하는가?", "sartre-camus-split": "사르트르와 카뮈의 결별에서 누가 옳았는가?" } },
      "es": { "title": "El existencialismo: Sartre y Camus",
        "objectives": { "existence-precedes-essence": "¿Nos definimos a nosotros mismos?", "bad-faith": "¿Se puede evitar la mala fe?", "the-absurd": "¿Cómo responder al absurdo?", "sartre-camus-split": "¿Quién tenía razón en la ruptura entre Sartre y Camus?" } }
