  {
    "title": "The Social Contract: Hobbes, Locke and Rousseau",
    "category": "philosophy-psychology",
    "subject": "Philosophy",
    "topic": "Political philosophy",
    "passage": [
      "Social contract theory asks why anyone should obey a government. Its answer is that legitimate political authority rests on an agreement, actual or hypothetical, among people who would otherwise live in a \"state of nature\" without government. The three classic versions disagree about what that state is like and therefore about what the contract creates.",
      "Thomas Hobbes, writing in Leviathan (1651) after the English Civil War, held that people in the state of nature are roughly equal, competitive and fearful. Without a common power, there is a war of all against all, and life is \"solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short\". Rational people would therefore give up their freedom to an absolute sovereign, whose power to keep the peace should be very nearly unlimited. Only if the sovereign threatens a subject's life may that subject resist.",
      "John Locke, in his Second Treatise of Government (1689), described a state of nature governed by a law of nature: people have rights to life, liberty and property, which they acquire by mixing their labour with the world. The trouble is the lack of an impartial judge to settle disputes. People consent to government to protect these rights, and a government that violates them breaks the trust and may rightly be resisted or replaced. His ideas influenced the American Declaration of Independence.",
      "Jean-Jacques Rousseau, in The Social Contract (1762), began: \"Man is born free, and everywhere he is in chains.\" He thought natural humans were not warlike and that inequality grew with property and society. A legitimate contract must let each person, uniting with all, still obey only themselves. That happens when citizens are ruled by the general will, which aims at the common good, as opposed to the will of all, a mere sum of private interests. Whoever refuses to obey the general will, he wrote, will be \"forced to be free\".",
      "Critics raise several problems. Most people never actually consented to their government, and Locke's appeal to tacit consent by living in a country has been doubted (David Hume compared it to a sailor carried aboard while asleep who \"consents\" by not jumping into the sea). Feminist and critical theorists, such as Carole Pateman and Charles Mills, argue that the classic contracts quietly excluded women and non-Europeans. Rousseau's general will has been read both as the root of democratic self-rule and as a license for majorities or states to override individuals."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "state-of-nature", "title": "Whose state of nature is more realistic?", "description": "Compares Hobbes's war of all against all with Locke's and Rousseau's accounts and defends one as closer to human nature.",
        "concept": "state-of-nature", "definition": "The hypothetical condition of people without government, used to justify the form government should take.",
        "facts": ["Hobbes describes a war of all against all, with life 'solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short'.", "Locke's state of nature already has natural rights and a law of nature.", "Rousseau held natural humans were not warlike and that society bred inequality.", "A strong answer notes how each picture of nature drives the kind of government it justifies."],
        "misconceptions": ["The state of nature was a real historical period all three described.", "Hobbes thought people are evil by nature."] },
      { "key": "limits-of-authority", "title": "When may people resist government?", "description": "Judges Hobbes's near-absolute sovereign against Locke's right of resistance.",
        "concept": "right-of-resistance", "definition": "Locke's claim that a government that violates natural rights breaks its trust and may be resisted or replaced.",
        "facts": ["Hobbes allows resistance only when the sovereign threatens a subject's life.", "Locke holds government exists to protect life, liberty and property.", "Locke's ideas influenced the Declaration of Independence.", "A strong answer weighs the risk of disorder against the risk of tyranny."],
        "misconceptions": ["Hobbes argued for democracy.", "Locke thought people may overthrow a government whenever they disagree with it."] },
      { "key": "consent", "title": "Did we ever consent?", "description": "Evaluates whether tacit or hypothetical consent can ground political obligation.",
        "concept": "consent", "definition": "The idea that legitimate authority depends on the agreement of the governed.",
        "facts": ["Few people ever explicitly agreed to their government.", "Locke appeals to tacit consent by living in and benefiting from a country.", "Hume compared this to a sleeping sailor carried aboard a ship.", "Critics such as Pateman and Mills argue the classic contracts excluded women and non-Europeans."],
        "misconceptions": ["Voting in an election means you signed the social contract.", "If consent is hypothetical, the theory is pointless."] },
      { "key": "general-will", "title": "Is the general will freedom or tyranny?", "description": "Interprets Rousseau's general will and 'forced to be free', and judges whether it protects or threatens individuals.",
        "concept": "general-will", "definition": "Rousseau's will of the community aimed at the common good, distinct from the sum of private interests.",
        "facts": ["Rousseau distinguishes the general will from the will of all.", "Obeying the general will is meant to be obeying oneself.", "He wrote that whoever refuses to obey it will be 'forced to be free'.", "A strong answer considers both democratic and authoritarian readings."],
        "misconceptions": ["The general will is whatever the majority votes for.", "Rousseau simply wanted a dictatorship."] }
    ],
