      "Plutarch records that the Athenians preserved the ship of the hero Theseus, replacing its planks one by one as they rotted, so that philosophers argued over whether it remained the same ship. Thomas Hobbes added a twist: suppose someone kept every discarded plank and rebuilt them into a ship. Which one is the ship of Theseus?",
      "The same question arises for persons. What makes you, now, the same person as a child in an old photograph, when almost every feature of your body and mind has changed? Philosophers distinguish numerical identity (being one and the same thing) from qualitative identity (being exactly alike).",
      "John Locke proposed a psychological answer: a person is a thinking being that can consider itself the same thinking thing at different times, and personal identity extends as far as consciousness, chiefly memory, reaches back. He imagined the soul of a prince entering the body of a cobbler, and argued that the person would go where the prince's consciousness went.",
      "Critics pressed objections. Thomas Reid's brave officer case: a boy is flogged, later as a young officer remembers the flogging, and later still as a general remembers being the officer but not the boy. By Locke's memory test the general is the officer and the officer the boy, yet the general is not the boy, which breaks the logic of identity. Later psychological theories answer with overlapping chains of memory and other mental connections.",
      "Others defend a physical or animalist view: you are a human organism, and you persist as long as that organism does. Thought experiments pull in different directions: if your brain were transplanted into another body, many say you would go with the brain; if a teleporter destroyed you and built an exact copy on Mars, many doubt the copy would be you; and if it made two copies, both cannot be you.",
      "David Hume doubted there is any self beyond a bundle of perceptions, and Buddhist thought has long taught a similar doctrine of no-self (anatta). Derek Parfit, in Reasons and Persons (1984), argued that identity is not what matters: what matters is psychological connectedness and continuity, which come in degrees. If so, questions about the ship of Theseus, and about ourselves, may sometimes have no deep answer. The stakes are practical: punishment for past crimes, promises, advance directives and fear of death all seem to depend on who is the same person."
