    "passage": [
      "Herodotus tells how the Persian king Darius asked some Greeks what it would take for them to eat their dead fathers, and they said no sum could make them. He then asked members of an Indian people who did eat their dead what it would take to make them burn their fathers, as the Greeks did, and they cried out in horror. Herodotus concluded, quoting the poet Pindar, that custom is king of all.",
      "Descriptive relativism is the observation that moral beliefs and practices differ between cultures and eras. Moral relativism, as a philosophical thesis, goes further: it says that moral claims are true or false only relative to a culture's (or an individual's) standards, and that there is no further standard by which to judge one culture's morality better than another's.",
      "Relativism has attractions. It seems to fit the facts of disagreement, to explain why moral arguments so often stall, and to express humility about judging others. The anthropologist Ruth Benedict argued that what counts as normal is culturally defined, and many people associate relativism with tolerance.",
      "Critics, among them James Rachels, argue that the move from disagreement to relativism is invalid: people once disagreed about the shape of the earth without the matter being relative. Much apparent moral disagreement may also rest on different factual beliefs, for instance about the afterlife, with shared values underneath. If relativism were true, we could not say a society that practised slavery was wrong, reformers like abolitionists or suffragists would be wrong by definition when they opposed their society's code, and there could be no moral progress, only change.",
      "There is also a problem about tolerance. If tolerance is valued only because our culture values it, a relativist cannot tell an intolerant culture it should be tolerant. Relativism also struggles to say what counts as \"a culture\" when people belong to many overlapping groups.",
      "Some philosophers defend more careful views. Gilbert Harman argued that moral judgments make sense relative to implicit agreements; David Wong has argued for a pluralistic relativism in which several moralities can be adequate while some are not. Their opponents, moral realists and universalists of various kinds, hold that at least some moral claims, such as that gratuitous cruelty is wrong, hold everywhere, while allowing that many customs, such as funeral rites, legitimately vary."
    ],
