        "facts": ["Herodotus' Darius story contrasts Greek and Indian funeral customs.", "Descriptive relativism differs from moral relativism.", "Disagreement about a question does not by itself show there is no fact of the matter.", "Some disagreements rest on different factual beliefs with shared values underneath."],
        "misconceptions": ["Because cultures differ, nothing can be truly right or wrong.", "Every moral difference between cultures is a difference in basic values."] },
      { "key": "reformers-and-progress", "title": "Can there be moral progress?", "description": "Tests relativism against reformers and the abolition of slavery, and judges whether 'progress' is meaningful for a relativist.",
        "concept": "moral-progress", "definition": "The idea that a society's morality can improve, not merely change.",
        "facts": ["On cultural relativism, reformers who oppose their society's code are wrong by definition.", "Relativism seems unable to call slavery wrong where it was accepted.", "Rachels argues relativism allows change but not progress.", "A strong answer considers how a relativist might reply."],
