    "title": "The Euthyphro Dilemma and Divine Command",
    "category": "philosophy-psychology",
    "subject": "Philosophy",
    "topic": "Religion and morality",
    "passage": [
      "In Plato's Euthyphro, Socrates meets Euthyphro outside the court. Euthyphro is prosecuting his own father for the death of a labourer, and he is confident this is pious. Asked what piety is, he eventually says it is what all the gods love. Socrates asks: is the pious loved by the gods because it is pious, or is it pious because it is loved by the gods?",
      "Restated for monotheism, the question becomes: is an act right because God commands it, or does God command it because it is right? Divine command theory, held in various forms by thinkers such as William of Ockham, says morality depends on God's will: what makes an act obligatory is that God commands it.",
      "The first horn of the dilemma threatens arbitrariness. If rightness is whatever God commands, then had God commanded cruelty, cruelty would have been right, and saying \"God is good\" would mean little more than \"God does what God wills.\" The second horn threatens God's sovereignty: if God commands acts because they are already right, then rightness exists independently of God, and God becomes a reporter of morality rather than its source.",
      "Defenders have offered several replies. Some bite the bullet. Others, following a line found in Thomas Aquinas, ground morality in God's nature rather than God's will: God's commands flow necessarily from a perfectly good nature, so God could not command cruelty. Robert Adams argued that obligation comes from the commands of a loving God, so that commands to be cruel are not merely unlikely but would not create obligation.",
      "Critics reply that this only moves the dilemma: is God's nature good because it has the features it has, such as love and justice, or are those features good because they are God's? If the former, there is a standard of goodness we could in principle understand without reference to God.",
      "The dilemma matters beyond theology. It asks whether anything, a god, a society, a ruler or majority opinion, can make something right just by approving it, or whether approval should track reasons that exist independently. Many people who are not religious still hold views with the same structure, and many religious philosophers accept that moral truths are knowable by reason."
    ],
