  {
    "title": "The Problem of Evil",
    "category": "philosophy-psychology",
    "subject": "Philosophy",
    "topic": "Philosophy of religion",
    "passage": [
      "The problem of evil asks how the existence of suffering can be reconciled with a God who is all-powerful, all-knowing and perfectly good. David Hume, in his Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion, put an ancient form of the question in the mouth of his character Philo: is God willing to prevent evil but not able? Then he is impotent. Able but not willing? Then he is malevolent. Both able and willing? Then whence evil?",
      "Philosophers distinguish moral evil, which comes from free agents (murder, cruelty), from natural evil, which comes from natural processes (earthquakes, disease). They also distinguish two forms of the argument. The logical problem claims that God and evil are strictly inconsistent. The evidential problem, associated with William Rowe, claims only that the amount and kinds of suffering we see, such as a fawn dying slowly in a forest fire unseen by anyone, make God's existence improbable.",
      "The most influential reply to the logical problem is Alvin Plantinga's free will defence. A world with free creatures who freely choose good is more valuable than a world of automata, but God cannot cause free creatures to always freely choose good. It is therefore possible that God could not create a world with free creatures and no moral evil. Many philosophers, including some atheists, accept that this shows there is no strict contradiction.",
      "Theodicies try to go further and explain why God actually permits evil. John Hick's \"soul-making\" theodicy, drawing on Irenaeus, holds that the world is a place where character is formed; courage, compassion and perseverance require real danger and need. Others appeal to the regularity of natural laws, which make free action possible but also produce disasters. Augustine traced evil to the misuse of free will and saw evil as a privation of good rather than a thing in itself.",
      "Critics answer that free will does not obviously explain natural evil, that the amount and distribution of suffering, especially that of children and animals, seems far beyond what soul-making requires, and that many people are crushed rather than ennobled by suffering. Skeptical theists reply that we should not expect to see God's reasons, given the gap between human and divine understanding; their opponents worry that such skepticism would also undercut ordinary moral judgments. Some, like Ivan in Dostoevsky's The Brothers Karamazov, reject any justification that makes a child's suffering the price of harmony."
    ],
