      "What makes a theory scientific? Karl Popper called this the problem of demarcation. As a young man in Vienna he noticed that Einstein's general relativity made a risky prediction, that starlight would bend near the sun, which could have failed and was tested during the 1919 eclipse. By contrast, he thought, Freudian psychoanalysis and the Marxist theory of history of his day could explain any outcome, and their supporters saw confirmation everywhere.",
      "Popper concluded that a theory is scientific if it is falsifiable: if some possible observation would contradict it. Following Hume, he held that no number of observations can prove a universal law (every white swan seen does not show all swans are white), but one genuine counterexample can refute it. Science advances by bold conjectures and attempted refutations, and a theory that survives severe tests is corroborated, never proven.",
