      "Artificial intelligence systems now help decide who gets a loan, which job applicants are interviewed, what news people see, and how cars and weapons behave. These uses raise ethical questions that philosophers, engineers and lawmakers answer in different ways.",
      "One set of questions concerns fairness. Systems trained on past data can reproduce past discrimination. A 2016 investigation by ProPublica into the COMPAS risk tool used in some US courts reported that Black defendants who did not reoffend were more often labelled high risk than white defendants who did not; the tool's makers replied that its scores were equally well calibrated across groups. Researchers later showed that, when base rates differ between groups, these two notions of fairness cannot generally both be satisfied. Choosing between them is a value judgment, not a purely technical one.",
      "A second set concerns responsibility and transparency. When a complex system makes a harmful decision, it can be unclear whether the developer, the deploying company, the user or no one is to blame, a so-called responsibility gap. Some argue people have a right to an explanation of decisions that affect them; others reply that demanding explanations could rule out more accurate systems, and that humans are not very transparent either.",
      "A third set concerns autonomy and power: surveillance, manipulation through personalized content, the concentration of capability in a few companies or states, effects on work, and lethal autonomous weapons, where many argue meaningful human control must be kept.",
      "Some researchers argue that the greatest risks come from future systems more capable than humans whose goals are not aligned with ours, and that this deserves urgent attention. Others answer that such speculation distracts from present harms to real people, or that it is too uncertain to guide policy. Many hold that both deserve attention.",
      "Finally, philosophers ask whether an AI system could itself have moral status, if it were conscious or had interests, and what we would owe it. John Searle's Chinese Room argument holds that running a program is not by itself understanding; functionalists disagree. Approaches to all these questions differ: consequentialists weigh benefits and harms, rights-based views set limits that no benefit can override, and virtue ethicists ask what the use of these tools does to the character of the people and institutions using them."
