    "passage": [
      "Euthanasia means deliberately ending a person's life to relieve suffering. Ethicists distinguish voluntary euthanasia (at the person's competent request), non-voluntary (when the person cannot consent, such as someone in a permanent coma) and involuntary (against the person's wishes, which almost everyone condemns). They also distinguish active euthanasia, such as a lethal injection, from passive euthanasia, withholding or withdrawing life-sustaining treatment, and both from physician-assisted dying, where a doctor provides the means and the patient takes them.",
      "Laws vary. The Netherlands and Belgium legalized voluntary euthanasia in 2002; Canada allows medical assistance in dying; several US states, beginning with Oregon in 1997, allow physician-assisted dying for terminally ill adults; many other countries forbid both. Refusing treatment is widely recognized as a patient's right.",
      "The main argument for a right to die appeals to autonomy: competent adults should be able to make the most personal decisions about their own lives and deaths. A second appeals to mercy: prolonging severe, unrelievable suffering against a person's will seems cruel.",
      "Opponents give several arguments. Some hold that intentionally killing an innocent person is always wrong, or that life has a sanctity that is not ours to end. Some rely on the doctrine of double effect: a doctor may give pain relief that foreseeably shortens life, if the aim is to relieve pain, but may not aim at death. Others give slippery slope arguments: legal euthanasia may put pressure on the elderly, disabled or poor to see themselves as burdens, and criteria may widen over time. Disability rights groups raise this concern strongly, and some point to the expansion of eligibility in places like Belgium and Canada as evidence; supporters dispute how much that shows.",
      "In a 1975 article James Rachels challenged the moral difference between killing and letting die. He imagined Smith, who drowns his young cousin in the bath for an inheritance, and Jones, who plans to do the same but merely watches as the child slips and drowns. If Jones is no better than Smith, then the bare difference between killing and letting die has no moral weight, and if passive euthanasia is allowed, active euthanasia, which can be quicker and more humane, should be too. Critics reply that intention, the doctor's role and the social meaning of a practice can matter even if the bare distinction does not."
